' VideoIdCodec — the Stremio episode id convention "{imdbId}:{season}:{episode}",
' the single translator between a season+episode and the string stream add-ons
' and the account library both key on. Previously the convention lived in three
' places (EpisodesStore.ResolveVideoId, LibraryStore's EpisodeWatched inline
' build, and StremioLibraryCodec's ParseLibraryItem split); a format change
' touched in one and missed in another would silently desync the ids a series'
' ordered episode list builds from the ids watched-state queries and decodes.
' This class is the one home: Build constructs a 3-part id, Parse reads one back
' (returning invalid for anything that is not exactly 3 parts — callers
' distinguish a bare movie id from a malformed one with a colon check).
'
' Pure string logic, no Roku APIs, so it behaves identically in the interpreter
' test harness and on a device. Constructed under MainScene scope wherever a
' store new() needs it (episode ordering, watched state, wire parsing), like
' WatchedCodec — never built inside a method, or the per-component scope of
' user-defined globals would make the constructor resolve to invalid when
' invoked from a screen.

class VideoIdCodec

    ' "{seriesId}:{season}:{episode}".
    function Build(seriesId as string, season as integer, episode as integer) as string
        return seriesId + ":" + season.ToStr() + ":" + episode.ToStr()
    end function

    ' { season, episode } for a well-formed 3-part episode id, invalid for
    ' anything else — a bare movie id has one part, and every malformed count
    ' (2, 4, …) also reads as invalid. The movie-vs-junk distinction is the
    ' caller's: after Parse returns invalid, a colon in the id means malformed.
    function Parse(videoId as dynamic) as dynamic
        if videoId = invalid then return invalid
        parts = videoId.Split(":")
        if parts.Count() <> 3 then return invalid
        return {
            season: parts[1].ToInt()
            episode: parts[2].ToInt()
        }
    end function

end class